module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.AmpleGlobalGeneration.Basic
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.LocallyFree

/-!
# Generation by finitely many global sections, and its closure under tensoring

Clause (7) of [Stacks, Tag 01Q3] asserts that a finite-type quasi-coherent module becomes
*globally generated* after a sufficiently positive twist. Its proof in the source uses, "without
further mention", that a tensor product of globally generated modules is globally generated
(Modules, Lemma 17.4.3, [Stacks, Tag 01AO]). That lemma is not in the repository and not in
Mathlib, and it is what this file supplies, in the finite form that clause (7) is about and for an
invertible second factor, which is the only case the twisting argument uses.

The predicate is `Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.IsFinGen`: `M` carries a family of generating
sections (Mathlib's `SheafOfModules.GeneratingSections`, i.e. a family of global sections whose
associated morphism out of the free module is an epimorphism) indexed by a finite type. The
sibling `Basic.lean` is where it meets the clauses of Tag 01Q3.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.IsFinGen`: generation by finitely many global sections.

## Main results

* `Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.IsFinGen.of_epi`, `.of_iso`: transport along an epimorphism and
  along an isomorphism.
* `Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.isFinGen_freeModule`,
  `Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.isFinGen_unitModule`: the free module on a finite index type,
  and the structure sheaf.
* `Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.freeProdIso`: `𝒪_X^{⊕ (ι × κ)} ≅ (𝒪_X^{⊕ κ})^{⊕ ι}`.
* `Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.isFinGen_sigma_const`: a coproduct of finitely many copies of a
  module generated by finitely many global sections is again one.
* `Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.freeTensorIso`: `𝒪_X^{⊕ ι} ⊗ P ≅ P^{⊕ ι}` for invertible `P`.
* `Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.IsFinGen.tensor`: **the tensor closure.** If `M` and an
  invertible `P` are each generated by finitely many global sections then so is `M ⊗ P`. This is
  the instance of [Stacks, Tag 01AO] that the twisting argument consumes.

## Implementation notes

**The proof of the tensor closure, and why it goes through coproducts.** From generating sections
`σ` of `M` the morphism `𝒪_X^{⊕ σ.I} ⟶ M` is an epimorphism, and tensoring on the right with an
invertible `P` is an equivalence
(`Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.isEquivalence_tensorRight`), hence preserves epimorphisms and
coproducts of every size. So `M ⊗ P` receives an epimorphism from `𝒪_X^{⊕ σ.I} ⊗ P ≅ P^{⊕ σ.I}`,
and it remains to generate that coproduct: `P` is generated by `τ.I` sections, so `P^{⊕ σ.I}`
receives the coproduct of those epimorphisms from `(𝒪_X^{⊕ τ.I})^{⊕ σ.I}`, which is
`𝒪_X^{⊕ (σ.I × τ.I)}` by `Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.freeProdIso`. Nothing here needs the
first factor to be invertible or quasi-coherent.

`freeProdIso` is `CategoryTheory.Limits.sigmaSigmaIso` — an iterated coproduct is a coproduct over
a sigma type — followed by `CategoryTheory.Limits.Sigma.whiskerEquiv` along
`Equiv.sigmaEquivProd`. `Stacks.AmpleGlobalGeneration.freeModule X ι` is the coproduct of `ι`
copies of `Stacks.Ample.unitModule X` definitionally, which
`Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.freeModule_eq_sigma` records, and that is what lets the two
coproduct lemmas be applied to it at all.

**Why `epi_sigma_map` and `epi_map_tensorRight` exist, and why they take their `Epi` hypothesis
explicitly.** `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules X` is a `def` unfolding to
`SheafOfModules.{u} X.ringCatSheaf`, and its `CategoryTheory.Category` instance is a different
term from the `SheafOfModules` one (its `Hom` field is `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.Hom`).
The two are equal only after unfolding plain `def`s, which `exact` does and instance search does
**not**: a hypothesis `Epi f` in one spelling is accepted by `exact` for the other and
is *not* found by `infer_instance` for it. So an `Epi` that arrives as a hypothesis — the `epi`
field of a `GeneratingSections`, which is stated in the `SheafOfModules` spelling — cannot be fed
to `CategoryTheory.Limits.Sigma.map_epi` or to `CategoryTheory.Functor.map_epi` directly. These
two lemmas bind it as an instance over a morphism variable of the ambient category instead, which
makes both spellings agree, and are the only reason the proofs below are not one line each.

**`Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.isEquivalence_tensorRight` duplicates
`Stacks.AmpleGlobalGeneration.isEquivalence_tensorRight` at an arbitrary invertible module rather
than at a tensor power.** The three lines are the same; the tensor-power version does not apply,
because the modules tensored here are not presented as powers.

No tag attribute is attached. [Stacks, Tag 01AO] is a statement about an arbitrary ringed space
and arbitrary globally generated modules — no finiteness of the index, no invertibility of either
factor — and only the finite, one-sided-invertible instance of it is proved here, so attaching the
label would overclaim it.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01AO](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01AO)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01B4](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01B4)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01Q3](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01Q3)

## What is not done

**Tag 01AO is not proved in its stated generality.** Two restrictions are in force: the index
types are finite, and the second factor is invertible. The finiteness is harmless for the
consumer — clause (7) of Tag 01Q3 is about finitely many sections — but it is a restriction, and
the infinite case would need `CategoryTheory.Limits.Sigma.whiskerEquiv` and
`CategoryTheory.Limits.sigmaSigmaIso` only, so it is cheap; the invertibility is used essentially,
to know that `CategoryTheory.MonoidalCategory.tensorRight` preserves epimorphisms and coproducts.
For a general second factor that is the statement that the tensor product of sheaves of modules is
right exact and commutes with coproducts, which needs the monoidal-closed adjunction of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/SheafInternalHom/Basic.lean` and is not extracted there.

**Nothing here says a module *is* generated by finitely many global sections except the free ones
and `𝒪_X`.** In particular no nonvanishing locus appears in this file; see `Basic.lean` for what
is missing on that side.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open AlgebraicGeometry CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits MonoidalCategory
open Stacks.Ample AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules

universe u

namespace Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration

attribute [local instance] Stacks.SheafInternalHom.schemeMonoidalCategory
  Stacks.SectionRingGeometric.schemeBraidedCategory

open Stacks.AmpleGlobalGeneration

variable {X : Scheme.{u}}

/-! ### Generation by finitely many global sections -/

/-- The free module is the coproduct of copies of the structure sheaf, definitionally. -/
theorem freeModule_eq_sigma (ι : Type u) :
    freeModule X ι = ∐ (fun _ : ι => unitModule X) := rfl

/-- An `𝒪_X`-module is *generated by finitely many global sections* when it carries a family of
generating sections indexed by a finite type. -/
def IsFinGen (M : X.Modules) : Prop := ∃ σ : M.GeneratingSections, Finite σ.I

/-- Generation by finitely many global sections passes along an epimorphism. -/
theorem IsFinGen.of_epi {M N : X.Modules} (h : IsFinGen M) (p : M ⟶ N) (hp : Epi p) :
    IsFinGen N := by
  obtain ⟨σ, hσ⟩ := h
  have : Epi (C := SheafOfModules.{u} X.ringCatSheaf) p := hp
  exact ⟨σ.ofEpi p, hσ⟩

/-- Generation by finitely many global sections transports along an isomorphism. -/
theorem IsFinGen.of_iso {M N : X.Modules} (h : IsFinGen M) (e : M ≅ N) : IsFinGen N :=
  h.of_epi e.hom inferInstance

/-- A free module on a finite index type is generated by its finitely many tautological
sections. -/
theorem isFinGen_freeModule (ι : Type u) [Finite ι] : IsFinGen (freeModule X ι) :=
  ⟨SheafOfModules.free.generatingSections ι, inferInstanceAs (Finite ι)⟩

/-- A coproduct of one copy of the structure sheaf is the structure sheaf. -/
def sigmaPUnitIso (X : Scheme.{u}) :
    (∐ fun _ : PUnit.{u + 1} => unitModule X) ≅ unitModule X where
  hom := Limits.Sigma.desc fun _ => 𝟙 (unitModule X)
  inv := Limits.Sigma.ι (fun _ : PUnit.{u + 1} => unitModule X) PUnit.unit
  hom_inv_id := by
    refine Limits.Sigma.hom_ext _ _ fun i => ?_
    obtain ⟨⟩ := i
    simp
  inv_hom_id := by simp

/-- The free module on a one-element index type is the structure sheaf. -/
def freePUnitIso (X : Scheme.{u}) : freeModule X PUnit.{u + 1} ≅ unitModule X :=
  sigmaPUnitIso X

/-- **The structure sheaf is generated by one global section.** -/
theorem isFinGen_unitModule (X : Scheme.{u}) : IsFinGen (unitModule X) :=
  (isFinGen_freeModule (X := X) PUnit.{u + 1}).of_iso (freePUnitIso X)

/-! ### A finite coproduct -/

/-- `𝒪_X^{⊕ (ι × κ)} ≅ (𝒪_X^{⊕ κ})^{⊕ ι}`: a coproduct of free modules is free on the product
index type. -/
def freeProdIso (ι κ : Type u) :
    freeModule X (ι × κ) ≅ ∐ fun _ : ι => freeModule X κ :=
  (Limits.Sigma.whiskerEquiv (f := fun _ : Σ _ : ι, κ => unitModule X)
      (g := fun _ : ι × κ => unitModule X) (Equiv.sigmaEquivProd ι κ)
      fun _ => Iso.refl (unitModule X)).symm ≪≫
    (Limits.sigmaSigmaIso (fun _ : ι => κ) fun _ _ => unitModule X).symm

/-- A coproduct of epimorphisms is an epimorphism. Stated with the epimorphism hypotheses
explicit: the two spellings of the category of `𝒪_X`-modules differ by an unfolding that
instance search does not perform, so an `Epi` carried as a hypothesis is not found as an
instance unless it is bound as one, which is what this lemma does. -/
theorem epi_sigma_map {ι : Type u} {A B : ι → X.Modules} (f : ∀ i, A i ⟶ B i)
    (hf : ∀ i, Epi (f i)) : Epi (Limits.Sigma.map f) := by
  have := hf
  infer_instance

/-- **A coproduct of finitely many copies of a module generated by finitely many global sections
is again so generated.** The generating index type is the product of the two. -/
theorem isFinGen_sigma_const {ι : Type u} [Finite ι] {P : X.Modules} (hP : IsFinGen P) :
    IsFinGen (∐ fun _ : ι => P) := by
  obtain ⟨τ, hτ⟩ := hP
  have := hτ
  refine (isFinGen_freeModule (X := X) (ι × τ.I)).of_epi
    ((freeProdIso ι τ.I).hom ≫ Limits.Sigma.map fun _ : ι => (τ.π : freeModule X τ.I ⟶ P))
    (epi_comp' inferInstance (epi_sigma_map _ fun _ => τ.epi))

/-! ### Tensoring -/

/-- Tensoring on the right with an invertible module is an equivalence. The tensor-power case is
`Stacks.AmpleGlobalGeneration.isEquivalence_tensorRight`; this is the same three lines for an
arbitrary invertible module. -/
theorem isEquivalence_tensorRight (P : X.Modules) [IsInvertibleModule X P] :
    (tensorRight P).IsEquivalence :=
  have : (tensorLeft P).IsEquivalence :=
    (isInvertible_iff_isEquivalence_tensorLeft X.presheaf (sheafCondition X) _).mp inferInstance
  Functor.isEquivalence_of_iso (BraidedCategory.tensorLeftIsoTensorRight _)

/-- Tensoring on the right with an invertible module preserves coproducts of every size. -/
theorem preservesColimitsOfSize_tensorRight (P : X.Modules) [IsInvertibleModule X P] :
    PreservesColimitsOfSize.{u, u} (tensorRight P) :=
  have := isEquivalence_tensorRight P
  Adjunction.leftAdjoint_preservesColimits (tensorRight P).asEquivalence.toAdjunction

/-- `𝒪_X ⊗ P ≅ P`. -/
def unitTensorIsoSelf (P : X.Modules) : unitModule X ⊗ P ≅ P :=
  whiskerRightIso (unitModuleIsoUnit X) P ≪≫ λ_ P

/-- **`𝒪_X^{⊕ ι} ⊗ P ≅ P^{⊕ ι}`** for an invertible `P`: tensoring on the right with `P` is an
equivalence, hence preserves the coproduct that the free module is. -/
def freeTensorIso (ι : Type u) (P : X.Modules) [IsInvertibleModule X P] :
    freeModule X ι ⊗ P ≅ ∐ fun _ : ι => P :=
  haveI := isEquivalence_tensorRight P
  haveI := preservesColimitsOfSize_tensorRight P
  PreservesCoproduct.iso (tensorRight P) (fun _ : ι => unitModule X) ≪≫
    Limits.Sigma.mapIso fun _ : ι => unitTensorIsoSelf P

/-- Tensoring on the right with an invertible module carries an epimorphism to an epimorphism,
with the hypothesis bound as an instance; see `epi_sigma_map` for why that matters. -/
theorem epi_map_tensorRight {A B : X.Modules} (P : X.Modules) [IsInvertibleModule X P]
    (f : A ⟶ B) (hf : Epi f) : Epi ((tensorRight P).map f) := by
  have := isEquivalence_tensorRight P
  have := hf
  infer_instance

/-- **Stacks, Modules, Lemma 17.4.3 for an invertible factor**: if `M` and an invertible `P` are
each generated by finitely many global sections, then so is `M ⊗ P`.

Not tagged 01AO: the source's lemma is for an arbitrary ringed space and arbitrary globally
generated modules, with no finiteness and no invertibility, and only this instance of it is
proved. -/
theorem IsFinGen.tensor {M P : X.Modules} [IsInvertibleModule X P] (hM : IsFinGen M)
    (hP : IsFinGen P) : IsFinGen (M ⊗ P) := by
  obtain ⟨σ, hσ⟩ := hM
  have := hσ
  exact ((isFinGen_sigma_const (ι := σ.I) hP).of_iso (freeTensorIso σ.I P).symm).of_epi
    ((tensorRight P).map (σ.π : freeModule X σ.I ⟶ M))
    (epi_map_tensorRight P (σ.π : freeModule X σ.I ⟶ M) σ.epi)

end Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration

end
